`Universities are often judged by how quickly their graduates find jobs, so it is understandable that some people want courses to concentrate on practical skills. I agree that employability should matter, but I do not think it should become the main purpose of every university subject.

There is a strong case for practical teaching in degrees that lead directly into a profession. Nursing students need clinical experience, engineers need to apply mathematical ideas to real designs, and computer-science students benefit from building working systems rather than only reading about them. Universities should also keep an eye on changes in the labour market so that graduates are not trained with tools that employers stopped using years ago.

However, a university education has a longer life than a first job. Specific software, regulations and business practices can change quickly, while the ability to analyse evidence, write clearly and learn unfamiliar material remains useful for decades. Subjects such as history, philosophy and pure science develop precisely these habits of mind. They also serve purposes that cannot be reduced to salary. A society still needs people who understand its institutions, preserve knowledge and question weak arguments, even when those activities do not produce an immediate commercial return.

The sensible approach is therefore not to turn universities into vocational colleges, but to make academic study less isolated from real life. A history student can learn research and data skills; an engineering student can study ethics and communication; and both can gain work experience.

In my view, practical preparation should be an important part of higher education, but not its overriding aim. Universities are most valuable when they prepare people for employment while also giving them the intellectual flexibility to cope with work and society long after graduation.`,
`Some students understand a subject best when they can talk it through with other people, whereas others need silence and time alone. Both approaches have clear strengths, and in my view the most effective students usually use them for different purposes rather than choosing one exclusively.

Group study is particularly useful when a topic contains difficult ideas or several possible interpretations. Explaining a concept to a classmate quickly reveals whether someone genuinely understands it, because vague knowledge is hard to put into clear words. Other students may also notice mistakes or offer a simpler explanation. In subjects that involve discussion, such as law or literature, a group can expose a learner to arguments that he or she would not have produced alone. The social side also creates accountability: it is harder to postpone revision when three other people are expecting you to arrive prepared.

On the other hand, studying alone gives the learner control over pace and attention. A student who is weak at statistics may need an hour on one problem while a friend needs only ten minutes. In a group, that difference can cause either embarrassment or wasted time. Individual study is also better for tasks that require sustained concentration, including reading a long chapter, writing an essay or practising recall without hints from others. Poorly organised groups can easily become conversations in which little serious work is done.

For these reasons, I would not describe either method as superior in every situation. I would learn new material and complete demanding practice alone, then use a small group to test my understanding, compare answers and discuss difficult points. This combination keeps the concentration and independence of private study while gaining the feedback that other people can provide.`,
`Using a phone while driving is illegal in many places, yet the behaviour remains common. The problem is not simply that drivers do not know the rule. It is largely caused by habit, overconfidence and the expectation that people should always be reachable. Reducing it therefore requires both credible enforcement and changes to the way phones are used in cars.

Many drivers have become accustomed to checking a screen whenever a message arrives. That reflex does not disappear when they get behind the wheel. Some also believe that a quick glance is harmless because they have driven for years without an accident. Work can add another pressure: delivery drivers, sales staff and managers may feel that ignoring calls means falling behind. When enforcement is rare, the immediate benefit of answering a message can seem more real than the possibility of a fine or a crash.

One response is to make detection more certain. Police can use roadside cameras where the law allows them, while penalties should be large enough to discourage repeat offences. However, punishment alone is unlikely to solve a behaviour that has become automatic. Phones and cars can help by switching on a driving mode that silences notifications and sends an automatic reply. Employers should also make it explicit that staff are not expected to answer messages while driving; otherwise a safety campaign conflicts with workplace culture.

Public education still has a role, but it should focus on the ordinary consequences of distraction rather than dramatic slogans. A driver does not need to be typing for minutes to miss a pedestrian or a sudden stop in traffic.

In short, phone use behind the wheel persists because technology, habits and social expectations encourage constant attention. The most effective response is a combination of reliable enforcement, safer device settings and a culture in which being temporarily unreachable while driving is considered normal.`,
`In many countries, young adults leave villages and small towns because universities and better-paid jobs are concentrated in cities. This movement can create serious problems for both sides, but I believe the advantages generally outweigh the disadvantages for the people who move, provided governments do not allow rural areas to be neglected.

The main benefit is access to opportunities that may simply not exist in a small community. A young person who wants to study medicine, engineering or design may have no suitable institution nearby. Cities also contain larger labour markets, so workers can change employers, specialise and build professional networks more easily. The move can therefore raise both skills and income. Some migrants later send money home, start businesses in their home regions or return with experience that would have been difficult to gain locally.

There are costs, however. Rapid migration increases pressure on urban housing, transport and public services. New arrivals may discover that a higher salary is largely absorbed by rent and commuting. Rural communities can suffer as well when the people who leave are mainly young and educated. Schools lose pupils, local businesses lose customers and an ageing population is left with a smaller workforce.

Even so, preventing young people from moving would address the symptom rather than the cause. A better policy is to spread opportunity more widely through regional universities, reliable internet, transport links and incentives for employers to locate outside major capitals. People would then have a genuine choice rather than being forced to migrate for basic opportunities.

Overall, urban migration gives many young people access to education, careers and independence, so its benefits are substantial. The disadvantages are real, but they are better managed through regional development and urban planning than by restricting mobility.`,
`Advertising has not disappeared, but many consumers are less willing to accept an advertisement at face value than they were in the past. This is mainly because people have more sources of information and far more experience of being marketed to. On balance, I see this as a positive development, although newer forms of advertising can be harder to recognise.

Before buying a product, a consumer can now compare prices, read hundreds of customer reviews and watch independent demonstrations within minutes. A polished television commercial therefore has to compete with information that may feel more credible. People are also exposed to advertising almost constantly, from social-media feeds to streaming services and websites. That volume has made many users skilled at ignoring obvious promotions, while ad blockers and paid subscriptions allow them to remove some of them entirely.

Greater scepticism is healthy because it pushes companies to compete on more than a memorable slogan. If customers can quickly discover that a hotel is dirty or a device fails after six months, a large advertising budget cannot completely protect a poor product. Consumers are also less vulnerable to exaggerated claims when they are used to checking several sources.

The difficulty is that advertising has partly responded by becoming less visible. Influencer endorsements, sponsored posts and personalised recommendations may look like ordinary opinions, particularly to younger users. A person who ignores a banner advertisement may still be persuaded by a creator whose commercial relationship with a brand is not obvious. Clear rules on disclosure are therefore increasingly important.

Overall, the decline in blind trust towards conventional advertising is beneficial. It gives consumers more power and rewards businesses that provide genuine value. The aim should not be to restore advertising's old influence, but to ensure that newer and more disguised forms of promotion are transparent.`,
`Governments have limited transport budgets, and both public transport and roads need investment. In large and congested cities, however, I agree that a greater share of new spending should usually go to trains, buses and other forms of mass transit rather than to constructing additional road capacity.

The strongest reason is that public transport can move far more people through a limited amount of urban space. A new rail line or high-frequency bus corridor can serve thousands of commuters without requiring each person to bring a private vehicle into the centre. Better services also give households a realistic alternative to car ownership, which can reduce congestion, parking demand and transport emissions. The benefit is not restricted to existing passengers: drivers gain when some journeys shift away from the road.

Building more roads can be necessary, but it is often a weak long-term answer to congestion in dense areas. Extra lanes may initially make a journey faster, yet the improvement encourages more people to drive or to travel at busy times. Eventually much of the new capacity is filled. Roads also consume land that could otherwise be used for housing, footpaths, trees or commercial activity.

This does not mean road spending should stop. Existing roads and bridges must be maintained, freight still needs reliable routes, and rural communities may have no practical substitute for road transport. In those cases, safety improvements and targeted upgrades can be essential.

The priority should therefore depend on location and purpose. In metropolitan areas where road space is scarce, I would direct most expansion funding towards frequent, dependable public transport while maintaining the road network already in place. That approach increases the number of people who can travel rather than simply increasing the number of cars a city can hold.`,
`Historic buildings can make a city distinctive, but not every old structure can or should be preserved forever. Some people therefore favour replacing ageing buildings with modern ones. I believe valuable historic buildings deserve strong protection, while ordinary or unsafe structures should be judged more flexibly.

The case for preservation is strongest when a building carries architectural, cultural or local significance. A centuries-old market, station or row of traditional houses gives residents a physical connection with the history of their community. Such places can also support tourism and local businesses. Once they are demolished, the loss is irreversible; a modern replica may copy the appearance but not the materials, workmanship or historical setting. Reusing an existing building can also avoid some of the waste associated with demolition and reconstruction.

Nevertheless, age alone is not a good reason to preserve something. Some buildings were cheaply constructed, have little heritage value and are expensive to make safe or accessible. Growing cities also need housing, hospitals and efficient workplaces. If every ageing structure is frozen in its current form, neighbourhoods can become unable to respond to changing needs.

A sensible compromise is selective preservation and adaptive reuse. Authorities can identify buildings of genuine significance, protect their important features and allow their interiors to be upgraded for modern purposes. Less important sites can be redeveloped, especially when a new project provides a clear public benefit.

In my view, the choice should not be framed as history versus progress. A successful city keeps the buildings that tell an important story while allowing the rest of its fabric to evolve. Careful selection is more realistic, and more valuable, than either preserving everything or routinely replacing the old with the new.`,
`Long working hours and high stress are often treated as personal time-management problems, but in many workplaces they are created by the way work itself is organised. Staff shortages, unrealistic targets and constant digital contact can make an ordinary job expand into evenings and weekends. Employers can reduce the problem by changing workloads and expectations rather than merely offering employees advice on coping with pressure.

One cause is understaffing. If a team loses two employees but its targets remain unchanged, the remaining staff have little choice but to work longer. A second factor is the 'always available' culture created by email and messaging apps. Workers may finish at six o'clock but continue answering requests for another three hours. In competitive organisations, people can also fear that leaving on time will make them look less committed than colleagues who stay late.

The first remedy is to measure workloads realistically. Managers should monitor overtime, hire enough staff for predictable peaks and remove low-value tasks rather than assuming that extra effort can continue indefinitely. Companies can also establish a genuine right to disconnect: messages sent outside working hours should not require an immediate response except in clearly defined emergencies.

Flexibility can help as well, particularly for workers with caring responsibilities, but it should not become a way of hiding longer hours at home. Managers need to judge performance by useful outcomes rather than by how long a person appears online. Regular one-to-one conversations can identify overload before it becomes burnout.

Ultimately, stress will not be solved by yoga classes or motivational posters if the workload remains impossible. Employers have the greatest influence when they design jobs with reasonable demands, adequate staffing and clear boundaries between working time and private life.`,
`Working from home has moved from a rare arrangement to a normal option in many occupations. It can create isolation and make collaboration harder, but for jobs that can genuinely be done remotely, I believe the advantages are greater, particularly when employees are not required to work from home every day.

The clearest benefit is the removal of the daily commute. Someone who previously spent ninety minutes travelling can use that time for sleep, exercise, family life or simply beginning work less exhausted. Remote work also gives employees greater control over where they live and can help people with disabilities or caring responsibilities remain in employment. For companies, recruitment is no longer restricted to people who can reach one office.

The disadvantages are mainly social and organisational. New employees may find it harder to learn informally when they cannot turn to a colleague beside them. Video calls are useful for planned meetings but poor substitutes for every spontaneous conversation. Home can also become an unhealthy workplace if employees lack a quiet room or find themselves answering messages late into the evening.

These problems are significant, but they do not require a complete return to office-based work. A hybrid arrangement can reserve certain days for teamwork, training and social contact while leaving concentrated individual tasks for home. Employers can also provide equipment and set clear limits on out-of-hours communication.

Overall, remote work offers substantial gains in time, flexibility and access to employment. Its weaknesses are mostly manageable through thoughtful working practices. For that reason, I believe the benefits outweigh the drawbacks in suitable occupations, although the best arrangement is often a mixture of home and office rather than either extreme.`,
`The desire to look young is not new, but it has become more visible and more commercial. People now spend heavily on skin treatments, cosmetic procedures, fitness programmes and products that promise to slow ageing. This is driven by social pressure and by the fact that youth is often associated with attractiveness and opportunity. I regard the trend as mostly negative when it turns normal ageing into a problem that must be purchased away.

Social media plays an obvious role. People repeatedly see edited photographs, carefully lit videos and advertisements featuring faces with few visible signs of age. Comparing ordinary appearance with these images can make perfectly normal changes seem like defects. Ageism also matters. In some industries, workers worry that looking older will make them seem less energetic or less employable, while the dating market can create similar pressure.

There is nothing wrong with wanting to look healthy or taking pride in one's appearance. Exercise, good sleep and basic skin care can improve wellbeing as well as appearance. The concern is the scale of the anxiety being created. People may spend money they cannot comfortably afford or undergo unnecessary procedures with medical risks. More subtly, an endless attempt to erase wrinkles can reinforce the idea that older faces are somehow unacceptable.

A healthier culture would distinguish self-care from fear of ageing. Advertising for cosmetic procedures should be honest about risks and should not exploit insecurity, while employers and media organisations can challenge the assumption that youth is automatically more valuable.

Overall, some spending on appearance is harmless and can increase confidence. The broader obsession with appearing permanently young, however, encourages insecurity and gives commercial industries a financial interest in making ordinary ageing feel like failure.`,
`Formal education is important, but beginning it as early as possible is not necessarily better. I disagree with the idea that very young children should spend most of their day on structured academic lessons. In the preschool years, play, conversation and social interaction provide a stronger foundation for later learning, although gentle exposure to books and numbers is clearly beneficial.

Young children develop through movement, curiosity and relationships. When they build with blocks, invent games or listen to stories, they are learning language, self-control, cooperation and basic problem-solving without needing a formal lesson. These abilities matter when they later enter a classroom. A child who can listen, take turns and persist with a difficult activity is better prepared to learn than one who has memorised letters but has become anxious about making mistakes.

Supporters of early formal schooling argue that children can absorb information quickly and that an early start may reduce educational inequality. There is some truth in this, especially where a good preschool gives disadvantaged children access to books, skilled adults and a rich vocabulary. However, this does not require turning preschool into a miniature primary school. A well-designed early-years programme can introduce counting, sounds and stories through play while still protecting time for physical and social development.

The transition to more structured teaching should therefore be gradual. As children become able to concentrate for longer and understand classroom routines, formal reading, writing and mathematics can take a larger role.

In my view, the quality of early education matters much more than how soon formal lessons begin. Very young children need stimulating environments and skilled teachers, but those environments should use play and exploration as the main route to learning rather than pushing academic instruction down to ever younger ages.`,
`When public budgets are tight, spending on museums, theatres and other cultural activities can appear less urgent than hospitals, schools or housing. Essential services must come first, but I do not believe this means governments should withdraw from the arts altogether. A modest level of public funding is justified because culture produces benefits that the market does not always provide on its own.

The argument for prioritising basic services is straightforward. If patients are waiting for necessary treatment or schools lack teachers, it would be difficult to defend an expensive prestige arts project. Governments have a direct responsibility to provide services that protect health, safety and basic opportunity, and these should receive sufficient funding before discretionary programmes are expanded.

However, art and culture are not simply luxuries for wealthy people. Public libraries, local theatres, museums and music programmes can be used by entire communities and help preserve a country's history and language. They also give children access to experiences that their families may not be able to buy privately. Commercial funding alone tends to favour activities with large audiences or obvious profits, so less popular but culturally valuable work may disappear without some public support.

The solution is not to place culture in competition with emergency medicine. Governments can fund essential cultural institutions at a reasonable level, require transparent budgets and use partnerships with charities, ticket revenue and private sponsors where appropriate. Large cultural projects should be judged against other public priorities rather than protected automatically.

Overall, health care, education and other necessities deserve the largest share of public resources. Nevertheless, eliminating arts funding would save relatively little while making cultural life more dependent on personal wealth and commercial taste. A responsible government can protect core services and still support culture in a measured way.`,
`Traffic congestion is usually blamed on the number of cars, but the deeper problem is that many cities make driving the easiest or only practical choice. Population growth, dispersed development and concentrated working hours add to the pressure. Governments can reduce congestion, but only if they address both the supply of alternatives and the incentives that encourage people to drive at the busiest times.

One major cause is car-dependent land use. When homes are far from workplaces, schools and shops, and public transport is infrequent, households have little reason to leave the car at home. Cheap or abundant parking makes driving even more attractive. Congestion is then intensified by the fact that schools and offices often begin and finish at similar times, placing a large number of journeys on the network within a narrow window.

Building extra road lanes may provide temporary relief, but it rarely changes these underlying choices. A more durable response is to improve public transport so that it is frequent, reliable and connected to where people actually live. Safe walking and cycling routes can replace shorter car journeys, while bus priority lanes can make public transport faster even when roads are busy.

Governments can also manage demand. Charging for the most congested roads, pricing parking properly and staggering public-sector working hours can encourage some journeys to shift in time or mode. New housing should be concentrated around transport links rather than added to distant areas with no alternative to driving.

No single measure will remove congestion. The best strategy is to make alternatives genuinely convenient and then price scarce road space in a way that reflects its cost. Cities that do only one half of this equation are likely either to inconvenience drivers without giving them options, or to improve public transport while leaving driving artificially attractive.`,
`People are having children later for understandable reasons: education lasts longer, housing is expensive, and many adults want to establish a career and a stable relationship first. Delayed parenthood can bring medical and practical difficulties, but within reasonable limits I believe its advantages often outweigh the disadvantages.

Older parents are usually in a stronger financial position than they were in their early twenties. They may have secure housing, savings and greater control over their working lives. This can reduce some of the stress associated with raising a child. Emotional maturity can also matter. Someone who has had time to build a stable partnership and decide deliberately that they want children may be better prepared for the loss of freedom and responsibility that parenthood brings.

The main disadvantage is biological. Fertility generally declines with age, and later pregnancies can involve greater medical risks. Couples who postpone parenthood for too long may discover that having the family they expected is difficult or impossible. There are also practical concerns: older parents may have less physical energy, and the gap between generations can become larger.

These risks mean that society should not present delay as cost-free. People need accurate information about fertility rather than assuming that medical treatment can solve every problem. At the same time, policies that make earlier parenthood more feasible—affordable housing, parental leave and accessible childcare—would allow couples to choose timing rather than being forced to wait for financial reasons.

Overall, later parenthood often brings stability, maturity and better resources, which can benefit both parents and children. The advantages can outweigh the drawbacks when the delay is moderate and informed, but postponing children indefinitely carries risks that career planning cannot completely control.`,
`Many developing countries promote tourism because it can bring foreign currency, employment and investment without requiring a large manufacturing base. Where a country has attractive landscapes, wildlife or cultural sites, visitors can create income relatively quickly. I believe this can be a positive development, but only when governments prevent the industry from damaging the resources and communities on which it depends.

Tourism creates work in hotels, restaurants, transport, guiding and construction, and it can support small businesses that would otherwise have a limited customer base. Visitor demand may also justify better airports, roads and public spaces. For countries with few export industries, the foreign exchange earned from international travellers can be particularly valuable.

The danger is that headline visitor numbers can hide weak local benefits. Resorts may import food and equipment, or send profits to companies based abroad. Jobs can be seasonal and poorly paid. Popular destinations may also experience rising rents, water shortages, waste and damage to fragile natural areas. Local traditions can become performances designed for tourists rather than practices with meaning for the community.

For tourism to remain beneficial, governments need rules as well as promotion. Environmental limits should be enforced in sensitive areas, local businesses should have access to licences and finance, and infrastructure costs should not simply be passed to residents. Tourism taxes can help fund conservation and public services, while training programmes can enable local workers to move into skilled and managerial roles.

Overall, developing countries invest in tourism because it converts natural and cultural assets into jobs and revenue. The strategy is positive when local people retain a meaningful share of the benefits and growth is kept within environmental limits. Without those safeguards, a successful tourism industry can eventually make a destination less liveable for the people who call it home.`,
`Parents clearly influence whether children learn self-control, empathy and respect for rules, so parenting programmes can play a useful role in preventing youth crime. I do not agree, however, that teaching parents better skills is the single best solution. Juvenile offending has several causes, and effective prevention has to support families while also addressing schools, neighbourhoods and the young people themselves.

Good parenting can reduce risk. Children are less likely to drift into serious trouble when adults know where they are, set consistent boundaries and respond to problems before they become entrenched. Some parents want to do this but are dealing with stress, conflict or their own difficult upbringing. Practical programmes on communication, supervision and non-violent discipline can therefore help, especially when support begins early rather than after a teenager has already entered the justice system.

Yet parents do not control everything. A well-supervised child can still attend a failing school, live in an area where gangs are influential or have few legitimate opportunities after leaving education. Poverty does not automatically cause crime, but persistent exclusion can make illegal income or group membership more attractive. Mental-health problems and substance misuse can add further risks.

For that reason, parenting support should form one part of a wider prevention system. Schools need counsellors and routes back into education for pupils who are struggling. Communities need safe youth activities, apprenticeships and credible employment opportunities. Young offenders who have already committed crimes may require mentoring, treatment and carefully supervised rehabilitation.

In short, stronger parenting can protect many children and deserves public investment. It is not a complete explanation for youth crime, however, and treating it as the best solution risks blaming families for problems they cannot solve alone. The strongest approach combines family support with good schools, early intervention and realistic opportunities for young people.`,
`Team sports and individual sports develop different qualities, so it is difficult to argue that one is universally better. Team games are especially valuable for cooperation and belonging, while individual activities give people greater control and can build self-discipline. In my view, the better choice depends on personality and goals, and people benefit most when they have access to both.

A team sport forces participants to work with others under pressure. A football player cannot succeed simply by being technically gifted; he or she must communicate, follow a shared plan and sometimes sacrifice a personal opportunity for the team. These experiences can be particularly useful for children, who learn how to handle disagreement, victory and disappointment in a group. Teammates also provide motivation. It is easier to attend training on a cold evening when other people are relying on you.

Individual sports offer a different kind of responsibility. A swimmer, runner or tennis player can measure progress against personal performance and often organise practice more flexibly. Someone who dislikes competitive group environments may enjoy exercise much more when success does not depend on fitting into a team. Individual training can also teach persistence because the athlete cannot hide behind stronger teammates when performance is poor.

Neither format is free of drawbacks. Team sport can involve conflict or limited playing time, while individual sport can become lonely and place all competitive pressure on one person. Good coaching matters in both cases.

For these reasons, I would not rank team sport above individual sport in general. Schools and communities should provide a range of options and encourage young people to try more than one. The main goal should be long-term participation in physical activity; the social lessons of a team and the independence of individual sport are both worthwhile routes to that outcome.`,
`Urban noise comes from many sources, but road traffic, construction, aircraft and late-night commercial activity are among the most persistent. As cities become denser and operate for longer hours, more people live close to these sources. Reducing the problem requires planning and engineering measures as well as rules about when and where noisy activities can take place.

Traffic is especially difficult because it is continuous. Engines contribute, but so do tyres, horns and heavy vehicles, meaning that replacing every car with an electric one would not make busy roads silent. Construction adds intense local noise, while bars, deliveries and entertainment districts can disturb residents at night. Airports create a separate problem because a single flight path can affect a wide area.

Governments can tackle road noise by reducing speeds on residential streets, maintaining smoother surfaces and directing heavy through-traffic away from homes where possible. Good public transport and safe cycling routes may also reduce the overall volume of car traffic. New buildings near major roads or railways should meet stronger sound-insulation standards rather than leaving residents to solve the problem after construction.

Time restrictions are appropriate for construction and commercial deliveries in residential districts. Nightlife areas need clear noise limits and consistent enforcement, but authorities should also plan mixed-use neighbourhoods carefully so that new apartments are not placed directly beside venues that have operated for years. Around airports, land-use planning and limits on night flights can protect the most affected communities.

Cities will never be quiet, nor should that be the aim. The practical goal is to prevent unnecessary and harmful exposure, particularly during sleeping hours. A combination of quieter streets, better building design and sensible regulation can reduce noise without stopping the economic and social activity that makes cities useful.`,
`Part-time work can either strengthen a student's education or undermine it, depending largely on the number of hours and the conditions of the job. In my view, the advantages outweigh the disadvantages when employment is limited and flexible, but not when students are effectively working a second full-time schedule alongside their course.

The most obvious benefit is income. Students who earn some of their living costs may need less family support or debt. Work can also provide experience that a classroom cannot. A student serving customers, organising shifts or working in an office learns punctuality, communication and responsibility. Even when the job is unrelated to the degree, these habits are useful to future employers. Managing a small number of working hours can also force students to plan their week more carefully.

The danger appears when employment begins to compete with the main purpose of being at university. Late shifts can reduce sleep, and twenty or thirty hours of paid work may leave little time for reading, assignments or office hours. Students from lower-income families are particularly vulnerable if financial necessity pushes them to work far more than classmates who can devote themselves to study. In that situation, part-time employment can deepen inequality rather than simply build character.

Universities and employers can reduce the risk through predictable scheduling, on-campus jobs and limits during examination periods. Students themselves need to treat academic deadlines as fixed commitments rather than fitting them around every available shift.

Overall, modest employment is usually beneficial because it provides money, experience and a connection with the working world. The advantage disappears when hours become excessive. A job of perhaps one or two short shifts a week can complement university life; a schedule that regularly leaves a student too tired to study cannot.`,
`Changing jobs several times during a career is now far more common than staying with one employer for life. This is partly the result of economic and technological change, but it also reflects a shift in what workers expect from employment. I see the trend as broadly positive because it allows people to adapt and find better matches, although frequent involuntary change can create insecurity.

Industries themselves are less stable than they once were. Automation, global competition and new business models can shrink some occupations while creating others. Workers therefore need to retrain or move between sectors. At the same time, professional skills are more portable: a designer, accountant or software developer can often take useful experience from one employer to another. Employees are also more willing to leave when they want higher pay, flexibility or work that fits their values.

This mobility has clear advantages. A poor first career choice no longer has to define a person's entire working life. Moving jobs can expose workers to new methods, broaden their networks and reward them for acquiring valuable skills. Employers also have to compete harder to retain good staff rather than assuming loyalty.

However, not all mobility is chosen. Repeated short contracts and redundancies can make it difficult to obtain housing, plan a family or build deep expertise. Constantly starting again may also be stressful, especially for older workers whose qualifications need updating.

The answer is not to recreate lifetime employment, which was never available to everyone. Governments and employers should make transitions less damaging through portable benefits, affordable retraining and fair notice when jobs disappear.

Overall, more frequent career change is positive when it represents choice and adaptation. The problem is not movement itself but insecurity without support. A modern labour market should make it possible to change direction without turning every transition into a financial crisis.`,
`Modern communication technology has changed relationships, but I do not think it has simply weakened them. Phones and social platforms can reduce the quality of face-to-face attention, yet they also make it far easier to maintain relationships across distance. The effect depends less on the technology itself than on how it is used.

The strongest criticism is that constant connectivity can interrupt the people who are physically present. A family may sit at the same table while each person reads a different screen. Short messages also make it possible to maintain a large number of low-effort contacts without giving much time to any of them. When people compare these interactions with genuine conversation, technology can certainly appear to make relationships shallower.

However, the same tools solve a problem that earlier generations could not easily overcome: distance. Relatives living in different countries can speak regularly, share photographs and take part in important moments by video. Friends who move away for work no longer have to lose contact. Online communities can also connect people with unusual interests, disabilities or experiences who may not find understanding in their immediate surroundings. These relationships are not automatically less real because they began or continue through a screen.

The more useful distinction is therefore between technology that supports a relationship and technology that replaces attention. A video call with a parent abroad can strengthen a bond; checking social media repeatedly while a friend is speaking can weaken one. Individuals and families can set simple boundaries, such as keeping phones away during meals or important conversations, without rejecting digital communication altogether.

I therefore partly disagree with the statement. Communication technology can encourage distraction and superficial contact, but it also allows meaningful relationships to survive circumstances that once separated people. Used deliberately, it is as capable of strengthening human connection as weakening it.`,
`International tourism can expose people to ways of life they would otherwise know only through films or social media, but it can also turn local culture into a product designed for visitors. Both effects are real. In my view, tourism can promote understanding, but only when destinations manage visitor numbers and give local communities control over what is shared and developed.

Travel has the potential to challenge stereotypes. A visitor who stays in a neighbourhood, uses local services and speaks with residents receives a more complicated picture of a country than a headline or online argument can provide. Tourism can also create economic reasons to maintain historic buildings, traditional crafts and cultural festivals that might otherwise receive little funding.

The opposite occurs when visitor demand becomes overwhelming. Shops that serve residents can be replaced by souvenir businesses, housing may shift to short-term rentals and traditional ceremonies may be altered to suit a tourist timetable. In heavily visited places, contact between residents and tourists can become less friendly, not more, because daily life is disrupted by crowds. Culture then risks being presented as a simplified performance rather than something that belongs to the community.

Good management can reduce these problems. Cities can limit short-term rentals, protect residential areas and direct visitors towards less crowded places. Tour companies can employ local guides and explain appropriate behaviour instead of treating a destination as a backdrop for photographs. Most importantly, residents should have a voice in decisions about development.

Overall, tourism does not automatically create either cultural understanding or cultural damage. Small-scale, respectful contact can broaden perspectives, while uncontrolled mass tourism can erode the very culture people came to see. The outcome depends largely on how tourism is organised and whether local communities share in both the decisions and the benefits.`,
`Most adults already know that exercise is good for them, so lack of information is not the main reason many remain inactive. The larger barriers are time, convenience, environment and habit. Effective solutions therefore need to make movement easier to fit into ordinary life rather than simply repeating health advice.

Modern work is often sedentary. Someone may spend eight hours at a desk and another hour commuting, then arrive home with family responsibilities and little energy for a gym session. In some neighbourhoods, walking or cycling is also unpleasant or unsafe because destinations are far apart and roads are designed mainly for cars. Cost can be another obstacle, although many forms of exercise are free. Finally, immediate comfort is more powerful than a health benefit that may be years away; watching television tonight requires less effort than going for a run.

One solution is to build physical activity into routines people already have. Safe footpaths, protected cycle routes and useful public transport allow walking to become part of a journey rather than a separate chore. Employers can support short movement breaks, secure bicycle parking and flexible schedules instead of expecting staff to sit continuously.

Exercise opportunities also need to be welcoming. Affordable local sports facilities, beginner classes and social groups can help people who feel uncomfortable in conventional gyms. Doctors can give specific, realistic advice—for example, beginning with a daily twenty-minute walk—rather than simply telling a patient to 'exercise more'.

Ultimately, knowledge is necessary but insufficient. People are more likely to be active when movement is convenient, safe and socially normal. Governments, employers and individuals should therefore focus on the design of daily life, making the healthy choice an easy routine rather than an additional task that competes with everything else.`,
`As populations age and retirement ages rise, companies are likely to employ more older workers. This change can create challenges around training and health, but I believe the advantages outweigh the disadvantages. A workforce that retains experienced employees while continuing to recruit younger people can be stronger than one that treats age as a reason to leave.

Older workers often carry knowledge that is difficult to replace quickly. An experienced technician may recognise a fault before it becomes serious, while a senior nurse may know how to handle situations that are not described neatly in a manual. These employees can mentor younger colleagues and preserve institutional memory when teams change. Keeping people in work also helps employers facing labour shortages and allows individuals who want to remain economically active to do so.

There are possible drawbacks. Some physically demanding roles become harder with age, and workers whose training took place decades ago may need support with new technology. Employers can also create frustration if promotion opportunities appear blocked because senior staff remain indefinitely. None of these problems, however, is an unavoidable consequence of age. Younger employees also need retraining, and physical ability varies widely between individuals.

Companies should design jobs around capability rather than stereotypes. Flexible hours, ergonomic equipment and gradual changes in duties can help people stay productive. Training should be offered throughout a career, not suddenly introduced when someone turns sixty. Mixed-age teams can combine recent technical knowledge with practical experience.

Overall, employing more older people brings substantial benefits in skill, continuity and mentoring. The disadvantages are manageable through training and sensible job design. The aim should not be to replace younger workers with older ones, but to build age-diverse workplaces in which people contribute according to what they can do rather than the date on their birth certificate.`,
`The move from school to university can be difficult because students suddenly receive far more freedom while facing more complex work. At school, teachers often set frequent deadlines and remind pupils exactly what to study. University expects students to plan much of this for themselves. Institutions can make the transition easier without removing the independence that higher education is supposed to develop.

The first difficulty is time management. A university timetable may contain relatively few classroom hours, which can create the impression that there is plenty of free time. In reality, students are expected to read, research and prepare independently. Large assignments also require work over several weeks, so someone used to next-day homework may delay until the deadline becomes unmanageable.

Academic expectations change as well. University students must evaluate sources, build arguments and use specialist conventions rather than simply reproduce information from a textbook. Large lectures can make it harder to ask basic questions, and students may be reluctant to admit that they do not understand.

Universities should therefore teach independent study explicitly. Orientation programmes can show students how many hours a course realistically requires, while first-year modules can break major assignments into stages such as topic choice, outline, draft and final submission. Libraries can provide practical sessions on research and referencing. Regular tutorials, office hours and peer mentoring give students somewhere to ask questions before problems become serious.

Support should gradually reduce as confidence grows. If universities organise every hour, students never learn independence; if they provide no structure at all, avoidable failure becomes a test of prior experience rather than ability.

In short, university study is harder because responsibility shifts from teacher to student at the same time as academic demands rise. Clear early guidance, staged feedback and accessible academic support can bridge that gap while still allowing students to become genuinely independent learners.`,
`Making public transport free sounds like a direct way to reduce traffic: remove the fare and more people should leave their cars at home. I support affordable transport, but I disagree that universal free travel is the best solution to congestion. Price is only one reason people drive, and in many cities reliability, coverage and travel time matter more.

Free fares can certainly help some groups. Low-income passengers would spend less on essential journeys, and occasional users might be more willing to try a bus or train. However, the new passengers are not necessarily former drivers. Some may previously have walked or cycled, while a commuter with a slow, infrequent bus route may continue driving even when that bus costs nothing.

There is also an opportunity cost. Removing fares for everyone, including people who can easily pay, requires substantial public funding. If the same money could provide more frequent services, dedicated bus lanes or new routes in poorly served areas, those improvements may attract more car users than a zero ticket price. A crowded free bus that arrives every forty minutes is still a poor alternative to a car.

Congestion policy should therefore combine better public transport with measures directed at driving. Cities can offer targeted discounts to students and low-income residents, invest in speed and frequency, and charge more appropriately for road use and parking in the busiest areas. Drivers then face a stronger reason to change while also having a practical alternative.

Overall, free public transport can improve access and may increase ridership, but it is not a complete congestion strategy. I would prioritise reliable, frequent and well-connected services, supported by targeted fare assistance and sensible road pricing, rather than spending the entire transport budget on making every journey free.`,
`Traditional culture deserves protection, but culture cannot be kept healthy by freezing it at a particular moment in history. Languages, food, music and social customs have always changed as people migrate, trade and adopt new technology. I therefore believe preservation and change are not opposites: societies should protect the knowledge and places they value while allowing people to adapt traditions to contemporary life.

There are good reasons to preserve cultural heritage. Minority languages can disappear within a few generations if children have no opportunity to learn them. Historic buildings, archives, music and craft skills may also be lost permanently when there is no support for the people who maintain them. Public funding for museums, language education and conservation can give future generations a real choice to engage with their heritage.

At the same time, forced preservation can turn living culture into a museum display. Young people should not be told that there is only one authentic way to dress, celebrate or form a family simply because previous generations did so. Traditions survive partly because people reinterpret them. A traditional musical style, for example, may reach a new audience when artists combine it with modern instruments. The result is different, but difference is not necessarily destruction.

The most sensible policy is to protect the conditions in which traditions can be passed on rather than dictating exactly how they must be practised. Communities can teach languages, record oral histories, support skilled craftspeople and conserve important sites while leaving individuals free to participate or not.

In my view, cultural change is natural and often creative, but loss is not always inevitable. Societies should preserve knowledge and access, especially where something could disappear permanently, without demanding that each generation live exactly as the previous one did.`,
`Plastic packaging is popular because it is light, cheap and effective at protecting food and other products. The same qualities, however, make it a serious waste problem when items are designed to be used for minutes and remain in the environment for decades. Governments have the greatest ability to change packaging systems, although consumers also have a role.

The first problem is volume. Single-use wrappers, trays and bottles create a large stream of material that is difficult to collect and recycle economically. When waste management is weak, plastic can enter rivers and the sea, where it harms wildlife and gradually breaks into smaller particles. Even when it is collected, recycling is not a complete solution because packaging often contains mixed materials, food contamination or additives that make high-quality reuse difficult. Producing new plastic also depends heavily on fossil raw materials.

Governments should begin by making producers responsible for the waste their packaging creates. Fees can be higher for designs that are difficult to recycle and lower for packaging that can be reused or processed easily. Deposit-return systems can improve collection of beverage containers, while unnecessary single-use items can be restricted where practical alternatives already exist. Clear national standards would also be more effective than dozens of incompatible local labels.

Consumers can support the transition by choosing refillable products, carrying reusable bags and containers, and avoiding excessive packaging when there is a realistic choice. However, responsibility should not be shifted entirely onto shoppers. A person cannot buy a low-waste version of a product if manufacturers do not offer one.

Overall, plastic packaging creates pollution because the system rewards convenience at the point of sale but passes disposal costs to society. The strongest response is better product design and producer responsibility, reinforced by consumer habits that favour reuse over constant disposal.`,
`Many cities are taking road space from cars to build protected bicycle lanes. This can cause short-term disruption and may be unpopular with drivers, yet in dense urban areas I believe the advantages generally outweigh the disadvantages if the lanes form a useful network rather than isolated fragments.

The most important benefit is safety. People are much more willing to cycle when they do not have to mix closely with fast-moving vehicles. Once routes connect homes with schools, stations and commercial areas, cycling can replace a meaningful number of short car journeys. This reduces demand for parking and gives residents a cheap form of transport that also provides regular physical activity. A bicycle lane can move many people through a narrow corridor while using far less space than the same number of private cars.

The disadvantages are most visible during the transition. Removing a traffic lane or parking spaces can slow some car journeys, and shops may worry about deliveries. Poorly designed schemes can create conflicts at junctions or place cycle lanes where few people need them. There are also residents who cannot cycle because of age, disability or the nature of their work, so cities still need accessible public transport and road access.

These problems argue for better design, not for abandoning cycling infrastructure. Routes should be continuous, junctions should be treated carefully, and cities can provide loading areas or delivery windows for businesses. Changes can be introduced in stages and adjusted using traffic and safety data.

Overall, reallocating some road space to bicycles is worthwhile because it gives people an additional safe transport choice and makes more efficient use of crowded streets. The benefit is greatest when cycling is treated as part of a wider transport network rather than as a symbolic project added wherever space happens to be available.`,
`People often say that success is the result of hard work, while others point to money, appearance and social advantage. Both views capture part of reality. Persistent effort is crucial because it develops skill and creates opportunities, but it operates within conditions that are not equally favourable for everyone. In my view, hard work is the most important factor that individuals can control, though it is not sufficient on its own.

Effort matters because most worthwhile achievements require repetition and tolerance of failure. A student does not become a capable doctor through talent alone, and a small business rarely survives because its owner had one good idea. People improve by practising, seeking feedback and continuing after setbacks. Hard work also compounds over time: skills gained in one project make the next opportunity easier to use.

Nevertheless, money and appearance can influence who receives those opportunities in the first place. A wealthy family may pay for excellent schools, allow a young adult to take an unpaid internship or provide capital for a business. Physical appearance can also affect first impressions in industries where image is important. More broadly, luck, health, discrimination and personal connections shape careers in ways that effort cannot fully erase. Telling every unsuccessful person simply to work harder ignores these differences.

That does not make effort unimportant. Resources can open a door, but they do not guarantee that someone will perform well once inside. Equally, hard-working people are more likely to benefit when education, hiring and finance are reasonably fair.

I therefore see success as an interaction between personal effort and external opportunity. Hard work deserves emphasis because it is trainable and largely under an individual's control, but society should not pretend that everyone starts from the same position. The fairest system is one that rewards sustained effort while reducing the extent to which inherited wealth or superficial characteristics decide who gets the chance to succeed.`
